A||N||5||AusFiero||Question for the Stereo Experts?||||5||
Z||000000||AusFiero||06-21-2001||07:34 AM||||Ok my mind got to active so blow the rest I need. Got a question for the stereo people out there. <P>Just something I am tossing around in my head and want to know how it would affect a subwoofers performance.<P>If a sub needed say 0.9 cubic feet of air space, this is an example for this theory.<P>Now lets say the sub was mounted inside a box say 0.4 cubic feet but was ported through 2 short but reasonably large diameter tubes into 2 other boxes either side of it being 0.25 each in size including the ducting.<P>What would this do to a subs performance? Would it be better to make the 2 other boxes ported or to leave enclosed?<P>Just asking cause I have a plan that may see up to 1.5 cubic feet of air space available if this would work. Well maybe more even as I am thinking of making behind the seat boxes to incorporate into my centre console sub woofer enclosure. Thought I might ask before I just went and made it and find it is an utter disaster. Cheaper to ask I think.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||06-21-2001||10:02 AM||||Well the general rule of thumb is the shape of a sealed box doesn't matter as long as the volume is correct. But I have built many sub boxes and in my opinion the shape has an effect. As for your idea, First the tubes would need to be a specific tuned size. To keep them short I'd guess they'd need to be 4" round and 2" long. If 4" round was too big for your design, then you could reverse them 2" round 4" long. Even though they are not a real "port" you would not want to distort the sound wave inside the box. If the shape of the box distorts the wave inside it will not properly apply equal suspension pressure to the back if the speaker. I would also use poly fill over the tubes, but not in the tubes. Given a rough size of 12X10X6 for the main box and 12X7X5 for the side boxes, It might sound fine. You would not want to port the box since a ported box is generally 80% bigger than a sealed box. You would also need to make the side boxes are "tuned". Not sure how running a port into a box then running a port out of the box would sound. In theory you are making 2 tuned bandpass boxes. So the size and shape of the side boxes will become more critical to the resonate frequency you set them for.<P>The only way you are going to really hear how it sounds is to build 2 boxes. 1 the way you want and the second a basic 12X12X12 box. Then install the woofer in one, listen to it then do the same for the other box. <P><BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-21-2001||10:16 AM||||only thing I would add is you might get a resonance at certain frequencies inside the tube length, so you might need to put something inside the far end to dampen the reflection.  <P>  isnt this how Dr. Bose got started?||||reg||1
Z||000003||AusFiero||06-21-2001||11:15 AM||||Thanks guys. Seems feasible enough to experiment with.<BR>You have also given me a great idea Oreif. I think I will build a sub test box that I can adjust the internal cubic size of easily and quickly. Not much use to me but I know a few stereo shops that would be very interested.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
